---
layout: layouts/shell.njk
permalink: /overlays/
title: Stream overlays
description: OBS browser source URLs and setup for the Mitchtopia overlays.
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
---
{% from "components/panel.njk" import panel %}

{# Read in a browser on the streaming PC, not loaded into OBS. Noindex so it
   does not turn up in search results. #}

<main class="landing">
  {% call panel({
    variant: "page",
    title: "Stream overlays",
    subtitle: "OBS browser sources",
    labelledBy: "ov-title",
    tabs: nav,
    currentUrl: "/site/"
  }) %}
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="section-lead">
        Each scene is a page on this site, so nothing needs copying between
        machines. Add a Browser Source in OBS and paste the URL.
      </p>

      <h2>Scenes</h2>

      <ul class="link-list" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem">
        {% set scenes = [
          { name: "Camera frame", path: "/overlay/cam/", note: "Just the frame with a shine sweep. Fills the browser source - size it over your camera." },
          { name: "Starting soon", path: "/overlay/starting/", note: "Full-screen holding card. Add ?mins=10 for a countdown." },
          { name: "Be right back",  path: "/overlay/brb/",      note: "Full-screen holding card, warning tone." },
          { name: "Ending",         path: "/overlay/ending/",   note: "Full-screen sign-off card." },
          { name: "Live",           path: "/overlay/live/",     note: "Full 1920x1080 scene: camera frame, lower third, clock." }
        ] %}
        {% for s in scenes %}
        <li>
          <a class="link-row" href="{{ s.path }}">
            <span class="link-row__text">
              <span class="link-row__label">{{ s.name }}</span>
              <span class="link-row__desc">{{ s.note }}</span>
              <span class="link-row__handle">{{ site.url }}{{ s.path }}</span>
            </span>
          </a>
        </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>

      <h2>Camera frame setup</h2>

      <p>
        This one is different from the others: it fills whatever size you give
        the browser source, so you size it to your camera instead of matching
        the full canvas.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Sources &rarr; + &rarr; Browser</strong>, paste
          <code>{{ site.url }}/overlay/cam/</code>.</li>
        <li>Set Width and Height to your camera's on-screen size - for a
          480&times;270 camera box, use 480 and 270. Matching them exactly
          keeps the border an even thickness on all four sides.</li>
        <li>Drag the browser source <strong>above</strong> your camera in the
          source list, then position it over the camera. The centre is
          transparent, so the camera shows through.</li>
        <li>Tick <strong>Shutdown source when not visible</strong>.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Frame options:</p>

      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>label</code></td><td>Caption text. <code>?label=</code> with nothing after it removes it</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>shine=off</code></td><td>Disable the sweep</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>speed</code></td><td>Seconds per sweep cycle, default 7</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>frame</code></td><td>Border thickness in px, default 4</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>corners=off</code></td><td>Hide the corner ticks</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <pre><code>{{ site.url }}/overlay/cam/?label=Mitch&amp;speed=9</code></pre>

      <h2>Full-screen scene setup</h2>

      <ol>
        <li>In the scene, <strong>Sources &rarr; + &rarr; Browser</strong>.</li>
        <li>Paste the URL. Set <strong>Width 1920</strong>, <strong>Height 1080</strong>.
          These must match your canvas exactly or the overlay is resampled and
          the text goes soft.</li>
        <li>Tick <strong>Shutdown source when not visible</strong> and
          <strong>Refresh browser when scene becomes active</strong>. The
          first stops an off-screen scene burning CPU; the second replays the
          panel animation and restarts any countdown each time you cut to it.</li>
        <li>Leave <strong>Control audio via OBS</strong> unticked - these make
          no sound.</li>
        <li>On the <strong>Live</strong> scene, drag the browser source
          <em>above</em> your camera and game capture in the source list. The
          camera shows through the transparent hole in the frame.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Customising without editing code</h2>

      <p>
        Every scene reads query parameters, so one page covers many looks.
        Change the URL in OBS rather than the source.
      </p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>title</code></td><td>Panel heading</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>sub</code></td><td>Line under the heading</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>banner</code></td><td>Banner text, e.g. <code>Notice</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>tone</code></td><td><code>notice</code>, <code>warning</code>, <code>danger</code>, <code>success</code>, <code>quest</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>lead</code></td><td>Body line</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>mins</code></td><td>Countdown length in minutes, starts on load</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>until</code></td><td>Countdown to a time, e.g. <code>2026-08-07T19:30</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>handle</code></td><td>Corner strip text (live scene)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>camlabel</code></td><td>Caption on the camera frame</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>clock=24</code></td><td>Force a 24-hour clock</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>Example - a fifteen minute countdown with custom wording:</p>

      <pre><code>{{ site.url }}/overlay/starting/?mins=15&amp;title=Back%20at%20half%20past&amp;tone=warning</code></pre>

      <p class="muted small">
        Values need URL encoding: spaces become <code>%20</code>, and an
        ampersand inside a value becomes <code>%26</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>Notes</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>The overlays are hosted, so updates appear the next time OBS
          refreshes the source. No files to copy to the streaming PC.</li>
        <li>Because they are hosted, they need a working connection. If you
          stream somewhere unreliable, use <strong>File &rarr; Save</strong> in
          the browser for a local copy as a fallback.</li>
        <li>Countdowns run from wall-clock time, so a throttled tab or a
          dropped frame cannot make them drift.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  {% endcall %}
</main>
